  "definitions": {
    "wall": "A whole equivalence component of supplied geometric edges under every declared dual segment through every supplied rank-two cell; the computation does not sample representative edges.",
    "wallGerm": "One endpoint of one geometric edge at a quotient vertex; the two ends of a loop are distinct germs.",
    "twoSided": "The signed edge-direction constraints across every dual segment admit a coherent {+1,-1} assignment on the wall.",
    "repeatedWallGerm": "A wall has at least two distinct edge-end germs at the same quotient vertex; this is reported before deciding whether a rank-two corner supports the pair.",
    "selfIntersectionAnalogue": "Two declared crossing dual segments in one rank-two cell belong to the same wall, or one dual segment pairs two occurrences of the same quotient edge.",
    "selfOsculation": "Two germs of the same wall meet at a vertex and that exact germ pair is not the corner of any supplied rank-two cell.",
    "directSelfOsculation": "For a two-sided wall, the two unsupported germs have the same in/out direction; global wall reversal preserves this classification.",
    "indirectSelfOsculation": "For a two-sided wall, the two unsupported germs have opposite in/out directions; global wall reversal preserves this classification.",
    "crossing": "Two distinct walls cross when declared crossing dual segments representing them occur in one rank-two cell.",
    "interOsculation": "The same pair of distinct walls crosses in at least one rank-two cell and has an unsupported pair of germs at some vertex.",
    "retainedRankTwoCell": "For a coorientation, a rank-two cell is retained exactly when its cyclic signed boundary has two sign transitions, equivalently one source and one sink.",
    "directedLink": "At a vertex, directed link vertices are edge germs pointing out or in; a retained rank-two corner is directed when every dual segment points away from or toward that corner. Feasibility requires both induced graphs to be nonempty and connected."
  },
